INT. FUNICULAR CONTROL HUT - NIGHT

A concrete box bolted to a mountainside. One window. Below it, the cable runs down into fog.

OKSANA VOLKOV (40s, lean, grease on her cuffs) stands at a bank of dials. A wrench in her fist. She hasn't blinked in a while.

DESMOND "DEZ" ACHEBE (30s, soaked parka, bad limp) sits on an overturned crate. A pistol rests on his knee. Not pointed. Not far from pointed.

Between them on the floor: a canvas duffel, zipped.

                    DEZ
          Stop looking at the brake.

                    OKSANA
          I'm looking at the gauge.

                    DEZ
          You're looking at the brake.

She lets the wrench drop to her side. Slowly.

                    OKSANA
          Car Two is stopped at the midpoint. Eleven people inside. Two of them are kids.

                    DEZ
          Then you better start it again when I say.

The radio on the desk CRACKLES.

                    MARGUERITE (V.O.)
                    (radio, tinny)
          Hut, this is Base. Oksana, come in.

Dez raises the pistol an inch. Oksana looks at it. Looks at the radio.

                    DEZ
          Say everything's fine.

She lifts the handset. Thumbs the key.

                    OKSANA
          Base, Hut. Everything's fine.

                    MARGUERITE (V.O.)
                    (radio)
          Your readout says Car Two is holding. Why is Car Two holding?

                    OKSANA
          Wind shear. Routine.

                    MARGUERITE (V.O.)
                    (radio)
          There's no wind at the summit station. I'm looking at the flag.

Dez stands. Limps one step closer. The pistol is level now.

                    DEZ
                    (low)
          Tell her to go home.

                    OKSANA
                    (into handset)
          Marguerite, finish your shift. I've got it.

Silence on the line. Fog presses at the window.

                    MARGUERITE (V.O.)
                    (radio)
          Okay.

Click. Dead air.

Dez exhales through his nose. Not relief. Arithmetic.

                    DEZ
          She's calling someone.

                    OKSANA
          She's calling the sheriff. Twenty minutes up the grade.

                    DEZ
          Then we have fifteen.

He nudges the duffel with his boot.

                    DEZ (CONT'D)
          Open it.

                    OKSANA
          I don't want to see what's in it.

                    DEZ
          Open it.

She kneels. Pulls the zipper. Inside: a hard-shell case, steel, handcuffed to a leather strap. She looks up.

                    OKSANA
          This is somebody's wrist strap.

                    DEZ
          It's somebody's problem.

A BOOT scrapes outside. Both freeze.

The door swings in. TARIQ BEN-HAIM (20s, uniform of the ski patrol, red jacket, wet to the skin) stands in the frame with his hands half raised. Snow in his eyebrows.

                    TARIQ
          Nobody told me the lift was down. I walked up the service road --

He sees the pistol. Stops.

                    DEZ
          Close the door.

                    TARIQ
          Okay. Okay.

He closes it. Stays against it.

                    OKSANA
                    (to Tariq, flat)
          Sit on the floor. Hands on your knees.

Tariq sits. Careful, slow.

                    DEZ
          Who knows you're here?

                    TARIQ
          My radio's on channel six. It's always on.

Dez looks at the red radio clipped to Tariq's vest. Crosses to him. Rips it free. Thumbs it off.

                    DEZ
          Now nobody does.

From the desk, the base radio hisses again.

                    MARGUERITE (V.O.)
                    (radio)
          Oksana. I can see the headlights on the service road. Two sets. Don't tell me everything's fine.

Dez turns. Slowly. Aims at the handset.

                    OKSANA
                    (quiet)
          Dez. If I don't answer, she calls it in as a hostage.

                    DEZ
          Then answer.

She picks up the handset. Her thumb rests on the key but doesn't press it.

                    OKSANA
          What do I tell her?

Dez's jaw works. Outside, far down the slope, an engine growls. Closer. Tires on gravel.

                    DEZ
          Tell her the truth. Tell her I want the car moving.

                    OKSANA
                    (keying)
          Marguerite. There's a man in the hut with a gun. He wants Car Two moving.

                    MARGUERITE (V.O.)
                    (radio)
          Copy. Then move it.

Oksana stares at the handset. Tariq stares at Oksana.

                    TARIQ
          Ma'am, don't. The kids --

                    DEZ
          Shut up.

                    OKSANA
          Marguerite, say that again.

                    MARGUERITE (V.O.)
                    (radio)
          Move it. Slow. Sheriff's at the bottom station. Moving the car puts it in his hands.

Dez's eyes go to the window. Headlights sweep across the fog. Then the glass goes white.

                    DEZ
          Don't.

Oksana puts her palm on the lever.

                    OKSANA
          You can still walk out.

                    DEZ
          With what?

He kicks the duffel toward her. Hard. It slides to her knees.

                    DEZ (CONT'D)
          Take it. Take the damn case.

She doesn't move. Tariq doesn't move. The cable HUMS under load, waiting.

Dez cocks the pistol.

                    DEZ (CONT'D)
          Pull it.

She pulls.

The whole hut SHUDDERS. Down in the fog, the cable begins to run.
